  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 311 AM EST Sun Nov 8 2026\n\nEarly morning GOES WV imagery shows a sharp trough digging into the \nCWA from the northwest with a ridge building upstream MT and the \nCanadian Prairie. Further Upstream a deep trough is landing over the \nPacific NW. A 998 mb low over far northern Ontario sends a cold \nfront across the UP today as it continues east. CAA and pressure \nrises yield windy conditions near Lake Superior with gusts in excess \nof 35 mph. Widespread gusts across the UP will otherwise be between \n20-30 mph. This also kicks up a marginal lake effect rain shower \nscenario for the east with minimal accumulations of a few hundreths. \nAlthough the forecast currently does not reflect this, wouldn't be \nsurprised if periods of lake enhanced upslope drizzle/sprinkles are \nable to squeeze out in the Keeweenaw and Michigamme Highlands. \nStrong winds across Lake Superior will build dangerous significant \nwave heights to 10-15 ft over the eastern shores. Otherwise temps \ngenerally hold in the 40s today, settling into the 30s to low 40s \ntonight under cloudy skies.\n\nA small and quick moving shortwave on Monday keeps cloud cover \ngoing. CAMs suggest this comes with a brief hitting rain shower over \nthe northern tier, but medium range guidance is not keying in on \nthis moisture. For now added PoPs mostly over Lake Superior, but \nthey do briefly graze the Keweenaw. Temps warm into the 40s to low \n50s. A weaker clipper system and associated shortwave travel to the \nnorth of Lake Superior Monday night into Tuesday, all precip remains \nto the north. A deeper trough following into Wednesday brings \nanother clipper with it, but what primarily introduces precip back \ninto the forecast is the colder airmass that accompanies it. 850 mb \ntemps drop to -8 C end of Wednesday, continuing through at least \nFriday, possibly through much of Saturday (20% chance). This keeps \nlake effect showers, mainly snow, through early next weekend out of \nthe north. Model soundings don't indicate much of any presence \nwithin the DGZ during this period or impressive moisture, but some \nlight snow accumulation is anticipated by the end of the week. \nProbabilities for 1 inch of snow by 12Z Saturday are 40-70% over the \nnorth wind snowbelts. Increase the total to 4 inches and it drops to \n20% at most. Otherwise temps return near normal with highs in the \n30s to low 40s and lows in the 20s to low 30s.",
